{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>The burden of Dumah.</b> Like ומשמע ודומה ומשא (Genesis 25:14)." "<b>One calls to me from Seir.</b> It speaks in the language of Dumah, as though he comes as a spy from Seir to scout the land of Dumah, and he asks the watchmen: Tell me, watchman, what of the night, what of the night. The meaning is as though the questioner is a man of Dumah and has need to arise at night to travel the road or to work in the field, as is the custom in most places, and the reason for the doubling is that he asks many. But the right interpretation in my eyes is that this phrase is to see whether the watchmen are asleep." "<b>What of the night.</b> It ought to be in construct state, and like it: ביין חלבון (Ezekiel 27:18), כיין הטוב (Song of Songs 7:10) — a place-name is elided. The Gaon [Saadia] says: שומר מה עבר מהלילה — שומר מה נשאר (watchman, what has passed of the night — watchman, what remains). In my opinion it is like בליל שודד עד מואב (Isaiah 15:1), and the meaning is: by night, thus far for the week."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "Resolved השד' as השדה based on context (working in the field); the abbreviation is somewhat unusual.", 2 "Resolved הגאון as Saadia per standard epithet convention; the final phrase מהשבור is textually uncertain and rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:54:35.818847368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}